{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788975786,"nonce":"2YT0tkp/NZFLecWAFpe8fF0OfvU=","title":"The collector re-traces a static live set on every collection","message":"binary_trees allocates ~900MB against a live set that is the SAME ~387 blocks\n(~12MB) at nearly every collection -- one long-lived tree, fully re-traced 18\ntimes. Marking is ~92% of GC pause (stop is ~0, sweep ~8%), and marking is\npointer-chasing, which is the most memory-latency-sensitive work in the program.\n\nThat is why ash is the most host-sensitive engine in the benchmark corpus: over\nsix CI sweeps the binary_trees median ranged 2.08x for ash-hybrid and 2.18x for\nash-aot, against 1.42x for the JVM and 1.86x for HashLink/C. The JVM's minor\ncollections trace only live young objects, a small and near-constant set, so its\npause does not scale with the host's memory behaviour.\n\nA generational (sticky mark bits) collector is the structural fix. It does not\nneed moving objects, which suits the existing non-moving Immix, but it does need\na write barrier for the remembered set, and that touches every backend\n(interpreter, Cranelift, LLVM, AOT, wasm).\n\nRuled out as the cause, so nobody re-checks: the parallel marker is not it.\nSerial and parallel marking are wall-clock identical on this benchmark and\nparallel is ~2% ahead on pause.","files":null}]}